# `where a, b` joins the conditions with `and` (M1-19).
mode: syntax
---- query
T | where a > 1, b < 2, c | take 1
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..35
  PIPELINE@0..34
    NAME_REF@0..1
      IDENT@0..1 "T"
    WHITESPACE@1..2 " "
    PIPE@2..3 "|"
    WHITESPACE@3..4 " "
    WHERE_OP@4..25
      IDENT@4..9 "where"
      WHITESPACE@9..10 " "
      BIN_EXPR@10..15
        NAME_REF@10..11
          IDENT@10..11 "a"
        WHITESPACE@11..12 " "
        GT@12..13 ">"
        WHITESPACE@13..14 " "
        LITERAL@14..15
          LONG@14..15 "1"
      COMMA@15..16 ","
      WHITESPACE@16..17 " "
      BIN_EXPR@17..22
        NAME_REF@17..18
          IDENT@17..18 "b"
        WHITESPACE@18..19 " "
        LT@19..20 "<"
        WHITESPACE@20..21 " "
        LITERAL@21..22
          LONG@21..22 "2"
      COMMA@22..23 ","
      WHITESPACE@23..24 " "
      NAME_REF@24..25
        IDENT@24..25 "c"
    WHITESPACE@25..26 " "
    PIPE@26..27 "|"
    WHITESPACE@27..28 " "
    TAKE_OP@28..34
      IDENT@28..32 "take"
      WHITESPACE@32..33 " "
      LITERAL@33..34
        LONG@33..34 "1"
  WHITESPACE@34..35 "\n"

error[DS0115]: `where` takes one condition
  --> query:1:16
  |
1 | T | where a > 1, b < 2, c | take 1
  |                ^ a comma does not join conditions
  |
  = help: combine the conditions with `and`: `a > 1 and b < 2 and c`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    T | where a > 1 and b < 2 and c | take 1
which parses without errors
